---
import ServicePage from '../layouts/ServicePage.astro';

const description = "Stop guessing what the ringing means. Get a clear clinical understanding of your tinnitus and what's driving it with a structured tinnitus evaluation at DUET Hearing Preservation.";

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalProcedure',
  name: 'Tinnitus Evaluation',
  procedureType: 'NoninvasiveProcedure',
  description,
  url: 'https://duethearing.com/tinnitus/',
};
const businessService = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Tinnitus Evaluation',
  serviceType: 'MedicalService',
  provider: { '@id': 'https://duethearing.com/#organization' },
  areaServed: { '@type': 'City', name: 'Lake Forest, Illinois' },
  audience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  description,
  url: 'https://duethearing.com/tinnitus/',
};

const allSchemas = [schema, businessService];

---
<ServicePage
  pageTitle="Tinnitus Evaluation | Duet Hearing"
  description={description}
  parentLabel="Hearing &amp; Tinnitus"
  currentLabel="Tinnitus"
  pageHeading="DUET Method – Tinnitus Evaluation"
  sidebarImage="/s9-card-2.jpg"
  schema={allSchemas}
>

  <p class="service-intro">Stop guessing what the ringing means. Get a clear clinical understanding of your tinnitus and what's driving it.</p>

  <p class="service-intro">At DUET, a tinnitus evaluation provides clarity, direction, and a personalized plan based on how your auditory system, brain, and nervous system interact.</p>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>You'll gain:</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A clinical understanding of your tinnitus pattern</li>
      <li>Insight into contributing hearing and neural factors</li>
      <li>A personalized care plan designed for daily life</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you've been told to "just ignore it," there is a more structured approach.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>Why Tinnitus Feels So Intrusive</h2>
    <p>Tinnitus is not only an ear condition, it is an interaction between the auditory system, brain, and nervous system.</p>
    <p>In many cases, it follows a pattern:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Changes in hearing input introduce an internal sound signal</li>
      <li>The brain increases attention to that signal</li>
      <li>The nervous system assigns it importance, increasing alertness and stress</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This interaction can make tinnitus feel louder, more persistent, and harder to ignore, especially in quiet environments, at night, or during stress.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>The Tinnitus Loop</h2>
    <p>Tinnitus becomes most disruptive when three systems reinforce each other:</p>
    <p><strong>Auditory input (ear)</strong><br>Changes in hearing can alter the way sound is encoded and delivered to the brain.</p>
    <p><strong>Neural processing (brain)</strong><br>The brain may increase focus on the tinnitus signal when it lacks clear external sound input.</p>
    <p><strong>Stress response (body)</strong><br>The nervous system can treat tinnitus as a signal of importance, increasing alertness, and emotional reactivity.</p>
    <p>When these systems interact, tinnitus can become more noticeable and harder to disengage from.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>How Tinnitus Impacts Daily Life</h2>
    <p>When the tinnitus loop is active, it can affect more than hearing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Sleep becomes more difficult to maintain</li>
      <li>Quiet environments feel more noticeable or uncomfortable</li>
      <li>Concentration requires more effort</li>
      <li>Conversations can feel mentally tiring</li>
      <li>Relaxation feels less complete</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These effects are not just about sound; they reflect how the brain and body are responding to it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>What a Tinnitus Evaluation Includes</h2>
    <p>At DUET Hearing Preservation, your evaluation is structured to provide clear answers, not general advice.</p>
    <p><strong>Tinnitus-Focused Intake</strong><br>We review your history, symptom timeline, triggers, sound characteristics, and how tinnitus affects your daily life.</p>
    <p><strong>Comprehensive Hearing Evaluation</strong><br>We assess how your hearing system is functioning, including subtle changes that may only appear in complex listening environments.</p>
    <p><strong>Tinnitus Analysis &amp; Questionnaires</strong><br>We evaluate pitch, perception, and functional impact using validated tools to understand how tinnitus is affecting you, not just how it sounds.</p>
    <p><strong>Clinical Screening &amp; Guidance</strong><br>If your symptoms suggest medical follow-up is needed, you receive clear, direct guidance on next steps.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>Your Care Plan</h2>
    <p>A tinnitus evaluation should end with clarity and direction.</p>
    <p>You will leave with:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A summary of your results</li>
      <li>The most likely contributors to your tinnitus</li>
      <li>A personalized management plan for daily life</li>
      <li>A recommended follow-up pathway to track progress</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>DUET Biologic Care Approach (Cortex Calm)</h2>
    <p>DUET's biologic care approach is designed to address how auditory input, brain processing, and stress responses interact.</p>
    <p>The goal is to reduce the reinforcement between hearing tinnitus and the body's alert system.</p>
    <p>Your care plan may include guidance for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Sleep and nighttime sound management</li>
      <li>Quiet environments where tinnitus is most noticeable</li>
      <li>Daily listening and sound exposure balance</li>
      <li>Reducing reactivity to internal sound signals</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The objective is to shift tinnitus from a high-priority signal to a background perception that no longer dominates attention.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>Why This Approach Matters</h2>
    <p>Tinnitus is influenced by more than hearing alone. Its impact is shaped by attention, stress physiology, and how the brain assigns meaning to sound.</p>
    <p>By addressing the auditory system and nervous system together, care becomes more targeted and more aligned with how tinnitus is actually experienced in daily life.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>Hearing-Based Support (When Appropriate)</h2>
    <p>If hearing changes are contributing, improving sound input can help the brain receive clearer external signals, reducing the need to internally "fill in" missing information.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <p><strong>How long is the evaluation?</strong><br>Most appointments last 60–90 minutes, including testing and a full review of results and next steps.</p>
    <p><strong>Can you identify the cause?</strong><br>You will receive a clear explanation of the most likely contributing factors and whether additional medical evaluation is recommended.</p>
    <p><strong>Is tinnitus curable?</strong><br>Some cases resolve. Many improve significantly. The goal is not just sound reduction, but improved sleep, reduced intrusiveness, and better control over attention.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="service-block">
    <h2>Schedule Your Tinnitus Evaluation</h2>
    <p>You've lived with the noise long enough to know it's not something to ignore.</p>
    <p>A focused evaluation provides clarity and a plan built around how you actually live.</p>
    <p>Schedule your tinnitus evaluation with DUET Hearing Preservation today.</p>
  </section>

  <p class="service-outro">Take the next step toward relief — schedule your tinnitus evaluation at DUET Hearing Preservation today.</p>

</ServicePage>
